From caf-talk Caf Feb 17 10:14:08 1993
Newsgroups: comp.org.eff.talk
Subject: Article 3--Re: Legal Tangles During FTP
Message-ID: <MORAN.93Feb16170552@muir.ai.sri.com>
Date: 17 Feb 93 01:05:52 GMT

[Note: events overtook the below message. An escalating stream of abuses
associated with our appearance in this group has just resulted in us
having to turn off anonymous FTP.  I decided to submit this message
anyway because it might still provide some perspective on the situation.]


Enough already.

A number of unfounded, unchecked assumptions and conjectures about the
anonymous FTP situation at my site have come to be treated as fact in
this newsgroup.  Up to this point, I have refrained from responding
because I was advised that the thread would quickly die out and the
falsehoods published here would be soon forgotten.  It has become
painfully clear that this is not happening and that the characterization
of our site is moving further and further from the truth.

If you are going to continue this discussion of anonymous FTP policy,
please create your own strawman (and start a new thread so that our
name does not continue to appear in the subject line).  Stop fabricating
actions on our part and using us as the bogeyman.

In an attempt to stop my site from continuing to be maligned in this
newsgroup, I am going to attempt to clarify the facts.  This message
is a long one because there are so many misconceptions, distortions,
and falsehoods to be addressed.  To keep the length manageable, I will
ignore many of the minor ones.

The thread started with a report by a student that we had overreacted
to an innocent use of anonymous FTP.  The facts are that the student
retrieved precisely two files: first the README stating that this was
not a public archive and then the (dummy) passwd file.  The fact that
he ignored a wide range of files in the pub directory is inconsistent
with the characterization repeatedly presented in this newsgroup of
an innocent browser who did not know that what he was doing was improper.
See our earlier response for a recapitulation of our actions - it was
posted to this newsgroup.  We deliberately underreported the event so
that if nothing else was going on, the student could easily claim 
innocent intent.  See the exchanges in the thread for additional
perspectives on this issue and why many System Administrators would agree
that what we did was reasonable and responsible.

Since so many people have expressed interest in our log of this incident,
here is the relevant extract:
  @<HOST> (<USER>@<HOST>) Thu Feb  4 06:18:58 1993
	get /pub/README
	get /etc/passwd
That's it.  All of it (except for the masking of the student's name and
originating host).  We log only the connection information and GETs and
PUTs.  Since we do not log CDs and LS/DIRs, we do not know what, if any,
filename browsing the student did. This level of logging is adequate for
our needs of identifying the origin of automatic retrieval programs and
of being able to provide our users with the service of telling them who
is picking up their files.
Note: although a number of people complained to this group about not
having seen this log, again no one thought to contact us and ask for it.

I find it instructive about the attitudes and biases of members of
this newsgroup that the student's account of events was so readily
accepted, and that no one attempted to contact us before rushing to
judgement.  In a situation where skepticism was clearly called for,
it was given only a perfunctory nod.  Saying "If <questionable_assumption>,
then ..." is often acceptable when debating abstract ideas, but it is
very wrong when dealing with someone's reputation.

Important note: several people in defending our actions have said that
it was not us, but the sysadmins at the student's university, who over-
reacted.  Be careful: the conclusion that they overreacted is based on
the assumption that they did what the student claims and that his action
was an isolated event.  I don't know whether or not this is true, and
I don't think any of you do either.  Furthermore, if the student's
university is governed by the usual set of privacy laws and policies,
the facts need to settle this issue are likely to be protected.  Thus,
if the sysadmins had good reason for acting as they did, they are
unlikely to be able to reveal those reasons. And if they didn't,
they can use "privacy rights" to hide that.  Repeating the point: don't
jump to conclusions based upon a presentation from just one side.

A related falsehood that keeps popping up is that we "defamed" the
student to computer facility staff, his department chair, and his
professor (or some variant thereof).  Our only contact was the single
report to the sysadmin of the student's site and the acknowledgement
that we got from them that the report had been received.  We made no
allegations -- we reported what was in our logs and noted the likely
interpretation (and identified it as such).  The complete text of this
message has been reposted endlessly in this newsgroup.

Members of this newsgroup have characterized our sysadmins are idiots
and incompetents for having a password file in the anonymous FTP area.
As was subsequently explained by other posters to this newsgroup, a
dummy passwd file is required by the standard BSD FTP server to support
the LS command (this requirement has long been documented in the FTPD(8)
manual page in SunOS).  Given how accessible this information is, I was
surprised at the number of supposedly knowledgeable contributors who
pilloried us for this, and I was disappointed by the number of people
who used it as an excuse to stage minor acts of harassment over the net.

The ridiculing of our site then moved on to what is an admittedly
poorly written README file.  It was written by an overloaded staff
member.  Anonymous FTP has long been on our list of things that needed
to be revisited, but since we had no complaints or other evidence of
problems, it has never made it to the top of the stack.  You should
understand that FTP is not a major activity at our site, and hence
it was very easy for this to drop between the cracks.

When members of this group encountered this file, they did not ask us
for a clarification or suggest replacing the file with a better statement.
Instead they posted uninformed conjectures to this newsgroup and then
proceeded to treat those conjectures as fact.

One such thread is that we could be using a poorly stated policy and a
questionably placement of the README file to entrap people into violating
the policy.  This is utter nonsense.  The only action we have ever taken
with regards to files in /pub is that when we detect that we are being
swept by an automatic retrieval program, we request to be removed from
the list of sites scanned.  We have been sensitive to this problem for
many years because a high visibility sight such as ours may get swept
nightly by multiple sites, disrupting the legitimate uses of our computers.

The other major thread of unfounded conjecture about our FTP policy is
that we were putting confidential files in the anonymous FTP area.
This is a gross distortion of our statement that those files were not
of general interest.  As an example, a scene-recognition project might
want to provide a collection of preprocessed images to researchers on
similar projects.  I see no harm in asking people who don't have the
programs needed to display those files not to retrieve them.  However,
it is very easy to explain the harm done by people retrieving megabytes
of files for which they have no conceivable use: they interfere with
the attempts of those who have a legitimate interest in retrieving those
files and they interfere with the other uses of our computers.  Please
do not claim that our servers have plenty of spare cycles at night --
our system statistics, and my personal experience, indicate that our
servers are typically busy throughout the night.

Our invitations to retrieve files are often widely distributed: Our
invitation to this newsgroup to pick up the two part response of Feb 8
is one example.  All we want to do is stop people from blindly snarfing
up everything available at our site.  Please do not criticize us for
an overly harsh statement of our policy -- harsh statements seem to be
what is needed to get people to pay attention.

We have been accused of violating our own policy by having a listing
in the Archie database.  One of those citing this listing states
 > When archie was first implemented, it automatically tried to access and
 > catalogue every FTP server it could find.  Many sites objected, since they
 > didn't really consider themselves "public access".  The archie maintainers
 > changed things so that it only catalogued sites that gave permission.
Didn't it occur to anyone that this process might be imperfect, especially
that files listed before the policy changed might not have been checked?

If the issue of Archie listings had come up, I would have supported
having an entry for a file of moderate to widespread interest as an
additional way of propagating the invitation to pick up that file,
assuming that the listing of specific files would not result in our
site getting listed as a public archive.  But that was before the
pummeling that our site took in this newsgroup.  Since the opinion of
people here seems to be that an invitation to take one file is an
invitation to take them all, I have asked the Archie database
administrators to remove all entries related to our site.  I would
have preferred not to have done this, but we don't need the grief.

We have been widely criticized, often harshly, for not having a more
sophisticated anonymous FTP configuration.  Because I know of many sites
with similar circumstances, I will explain why much of this criticism
is off-base.
(1). We are a small site with limited resources, and anonymous FTP is
a low priority.  Our group is composed of 50 people, most of whom are
researchers.  Our computer facility staff is composed of one operator
and one system administrator.  Two researchers (me being one) share
the management of the facility and handle various support tasks as _part_
of our professional duties.  Several other researchers are available to
help in emergencies.
(2).  Many of the people whom we invite to retrieve files from us are
infrequent and inexperienced users of FTP.  Reminders about basics such
as binary mode and explanations about compressed files are often needed.
The current configuration already confuses some of these people.  By
keeping it as simple and as generic as we can, we make it easier for the
system staff at the guest's site to help them with their problems.
(3). The reason why guest accounts are often impractical has been
explained by number of other contributors to the thread.
(4). Other aspects of trade-offs between services to our users and
accessibility from the net were discussed in the second part of our
first submission to this group.
In summary, because anonymous FTP is a low priority for our site, we
attempted to provide this service with a minimum expenditure of system
staff time.  The README file was intended to keep inadvertent misuse
of our hospitality to a tolerable level.  Until last week, this had been
entirely adequate.

The issue of UW FTPD deserves special comment.  A number of submitters
to this group have taken our decision to not use UW FTPD as evidence
that we were inept, incompetent, negligent, or maliciously attempting
to entrap people into violating our access policy.  People should
remember that UW FTPD is advertised as still being in the beta test
phase (or so says the README file in the copy I retrieved in January).
My site has enough problem with beta test software that we are forced
to use on our primary tasks.  I saw no need to install beta software
for a low priority, peripheral task, especially when there had been
absolutely no dissatisfaction with the current system.
(Note: the software that we are currently using is the standard BSD
FTPD with an extension that we had acquired from the net several years
ago).


The result of this "discussion" was counterproductive 
-----------------------------------------------------

People should be aware that the effects of the drubbing administered
in this newsgroup are almost entirely contrary to the alleged goals
of EFF.

At the simplest level, we have had to divert computer facility staff
from productive work to respond to the allegations disseminated by
this newsgroup and to deal with apparently related actions. Consequently,
we have not had the time to fix the disputed README file.

We will be installing the UW FTP server.  I do not view this as a good
thing because its biggest "advantage" over our current setup is that
it enables us to deny access from other sites, and having the capability
means that it will eventually be used.  Members of this newsgroup have
made me painfully aware that attempting to rely on an "honor system"
will not only invite (incite?) abuse, but will invite public ridicule.

Our site was one of the earliest members of the ARPAnet.  We take pride
in going out of our way to make resources available to the Internet
community.  Because of our high visibility on the network and because
of our close associations with various universities, we are the
frequent target of a variety of attacks across the net.  The cost to us
of these attacks is hardly insubstantial, but our computer facility
staff and our center's management have _chosen_ to bear these costs
in order to maintain our accessibility from the net, especially from
universities.  This has not been an easy choice.  I know of too many
sites that have crumpled under the financial pressures and eliminated
virtually all access, except mail, from a variety of top-level domains
(eg EDU).  But the financial burden is not the only pressures on us to
reduce network access: there are pressures from collaborators' sites.

Because of recent events (some apparently connected with our appearance
in this newsgroup), the installation of the tcp_wrapper package has
been raised from the category of "when we have time" (translation: never)
to a top priority.  I regard this with trepidation because I will be
taking away one of my management's best excuses for doing nothing when
it is under pressure to reduce network access.  When we have been under
attack or threat of attack, my management would ask "what would it take
to better protect ourselves."  Until now, I could report that blocking
network access from those sites would require installing and testing
new software, to which the response has always been "Your time is better
spent elsewhere".  With the installation of this package, my answer will
become "One quick edit to a file" and his only responsible decision will
be "Do it".  At first, these limitations will be temporary, but temporary
expediencies that get used repeatedly have a tendency to become permanent.

Before the thread started a week ago, if you had asked me whether I
anticipated my site imposing any noticeable restrictions on access from
the net, I would have confidently answered that I did not. Today, I
think the question is not "whether" but "how soon".  If and when this
happens, remember that we did not go willingly -- some members of this
newsgroup played a significant role by pushing us onto the slippery
slope.

